---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Ornament from '../../components/Ornament.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedPages from '../../components/RelatedPages.astro';
import GetTheApp from '../../components/GetTheApp.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const title = 'What to Do After 75 Hard: A Daily Challenge That Scales | CardHabit';
const description = 'After 75 Hard ends, or ends you, there are three real options: run it again, drop to 75 Soft, or switch to a daily challenge that scales. CardHabit keeps the discipline without the burnout: five habit cards each morning, a binder of finished cards, a system that survives a missed day.';

// Content last substantively changed: November 2026. Update by hand when the page changes.
const updated = 'November 2026';

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    q: 'What should I do after finishing 75 Hard?',
    a: 'You have three real options: run the program again, drop to 75 Soft, or switch to a daily challenge that scales to the day you actually have. CardHabit is the third option: it deals you five habit cards each morning and you commit to what you can handle, so the days stay hard without the all-or-nothing math.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a 75 Hard alternative that is not easier?',
    a: 'Yes. Most alternatives, including 75 Soft, lower the bar. CardHabit keeps the bar and changes the shape. Every morning it deals you five habit cards. You commit before your day starts, every card you finish goes into your binder, and a missed day ends a streak instead of erasing weeks of work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I do 75 Hard with friends?',
    a: '75 Hard is solo by design; you can start on the same day as a friend, but the program has no mechanic connecting you. CardHabit 2.0 builds friends into the rules with Deal a friend in: from any card in your hand, you deal the same card into a friend\'s hand for today, by share link if they don\'t have the app. If you both finish it, you each get a paired foil card with both names on it. If one of you doesn\'t, nothing bad happens.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: pageFaqs.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const h2Style = "font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-weight:500;font-size:2rem;color:#2B211B;margin:0 0 1.5rem;";
const h3Style = "font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-weight:500;font-size:1.4rem;color:#6E1E22;margin:0 0 0.5rem;";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs section="Who it is for" name="For 75 Hard quitters" />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  <Nav />
  <main>
    <section style="padding:5rem 1.5rem 3rem;text-align:center;">
      <div style="max-width:780px;margin:0 auto;">
        <p style="font-family:'Jost',system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:10px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:0.28em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6E1E22;margin:0 0 1.25rem;">
          For 75 Hard quitters
        </p>
        <h1 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-weight:500;font-size:clamp(2.4rem,7vw,3.75rem);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-0.015em;color:#2B211B;margin:0 0 1.75rem;">
          You didn't fail. <em style="color:#6E1E22;font-style:italic;">The math did.</em>
        </h1>
        <p style="font-family:'Jost',system-ui,sans-serif;font-weight:300;font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.7;color:#574B40;max-width:640px;margin:0 auto;">
          Two 45-minute workouts, a gallon of water, a diet with no cheat days, ten pages, a progress photo. Every day. Miss one requirement on Day 61 and you start over at Day 1. That's not a discipline problem. That's a design that was never built to survive a bad Tuesday.
        </p>
        <p style="font-family:'Jost',system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:10px;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#645749;margin:1.5rem 0 0;">
          Updated {updated}
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Ornament />

    <section style="padding:3rem 1.5rem;">
      <div style="max-width:780px;margin:0 auto;font-family:'Jost',system-ui,sans-serif;font-weight:300;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.75;color:#574B40;">
        <h2 style={h2Style}>What to do after 75 Hard</h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1rem;">You have three real options: run the program again, drop to 75 Soft, or switch to a daily challenge that scales to the day you actually have. CardHabit is the third option. It deals you five habit cards each morning and you commit to what you can handle, so the days stay hard without the all-or-nothing math.</p>
        <p style="margin:0 0 2rem;">The mechanic in one sentence: every morning CardHabit deals you five habit cards, you commit to the ones you can handle, and every card you finish goes into your binder, a collection of the days you showed up.</p>

        <h3 style={h3Style}>Option one: run it again</h3>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1.5rem;">Some people restart the day after a miss and finish on the second or third attempt. If proving the ceiling matters more to you than holding it, this is a legitimate choice. Know what you're buying: another 75 days of the same fixed rules, and the same cliff at Day 76 when the program ends and nothing replaces it.</p>

        <h3 style={h3Style}>Option two: drop to 75 Soft</h3>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1.5rem;">75 Soft trades the two workouts for one, allows a drink or a cheat meal, and removes the restart rule. It's more humane, and for a lot of people it's the right off-ramp. But be honest about the trade. It answers burnout by lowering the bar, and if 75 Hard taught you anything, it's that you don't actually want a lower bar. You want a bar you can hold every day.</p>

        <h3 style={h3Style}>Option three: a daily challenge that scales</h3>
        <p style="margin:0 0 2rem;">This is where CardHabit sits. The bar stays high; the shape changes. Instead of one fixed list for 75 straight days, the morning deal gives you a fresh hand, and you decide what you can hold before your day starts. Hard days and easy days get different hands. The discipline is the same one 75 Hard asked for. The math finally works.</p>

        <h2 style={h2Style}>What 75 Hard actually proved</h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1rem;">Whether you finished or not, you learned something true: you can do hard things on purpose. That's not nothing. Most people never test it. The program's binary structure, restart from Day 1 on any miss, was the point while you were in it. It's also why almost nobody keeps the program running past the 75 days. The system was built to prove a ceiling exists, not to hold you at that ceiling for year two.</p>
        <p style="margin:0 0 2rem;">If you're reading this because you're mid-restart, or because you finished once and the habits quietly evaporated by October, the problem was never your discipline. It was a system with no room for a human life.</p>

        <h2 style={h2Style}>What CardHabit does instead</h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1.5rem;">Every morning CardHabit deals you five habit cards at random (with CardHabit 2.0, from five suits: Physical, Mind, Nutrition, Recovery, and Connection, plus the Joker). You commit to the cards you can handle, not everything on the list. Cards are scoped on purpose: small enough to actually do before your day starts, hard enough to feel real. Hold a card down for a second and a half to complete it.</p>
        <p style="margin:0 0 1.5rem;">Miss a day and your streak ends. That's it. No restart from zero, no purity test, no photo audit. A new streak starts the next morning, because a missed Tuesday isn't a referendum on your character. The cards you already finished stay in your binder.</p>
        <p style="margin:0 0 2rem;">The company is real, just aimed differently. With CardHabit 2.0, say your friend Alex is holding a card for a cold shower. He deals you in, and the same card lands in your hand for today. If you both finish it, you each get a paired foil card with both names on it; if one of you doesn't, nothing bad happens. It lands as an invitation from a friend, not an order from a drill sergeant. You show up and do the card anyway, which is the same discipline 75 Hard asked for, minus the cliff at Day 76.</p>

        <h2 style={h2Style}>Hard things every day. Not extreme things, once.</h2>
        <p style="margin:0;">You don't need another 75-day program to prove you can push. You already know. What you need is a system built to survive the years after the program ends. CardHabit is the version of hard that lasts.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Ornament />

    <section style="padding:3rem 1.5rem;">
      <div style="max-width:780px;margin:0 auto;font-family:'Jost',system-ui,sans-serif;font-weight:300;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.75;color:#574B40;">
        <h2 style={h2Style}>Questions people ask at Day 76</h2>
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin:0 0 1.75rem;">
            <h3 style={h3Style}>{item.q}</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <Ornament />

    <RelatedPages current="/for/75-hard-quitters" />

    <Ornament />

    <GetTheApp source="for-75hard-quitters" />
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
